### VIII. STORAGE AND STATE MANAGEMENT

**Challenge**: Full chain history for billions of users → petabytes of data (unsustainable)

**Solutions**:

1. **Pruning & Checkpointing** (`PruningManager`):
   - Old transactions summarized into Merkle roots every 10,000 blocks
   - Only recent transactions kept in full detail
   - Checkpoints are immutable snapshots of state
   - Reduces storage from TB to GB per node

2. **Stateless Clients** (`StatelessClientSupport`):
   - Light wallets don't store entire blockchain
   - Instead, carry state proofs (Merkle branches proving account balance)
   - Query full nodes only when making transactions
   - Enables wallet on any device (smartphones, feature phones via USSD)

3. **Cold Storage Nodes** (`ArchiveNodeCoordinator`):
   - Not all nodes keep full history
   - Designated archive nodes maintain complete records
   - Universities, research institutions, national archives run these
   - Regular nodes can query archives when historical data needed

4. **Decentralized Data Availability** (`MerkleProofGenerator`):
   - Large payloads (receipts, audit logs, oracle data) stored on IPFS/Arweave
   - Only Merkle root stored on-chain
   - Anyone can verify data integrity using cryptographic proofs
   - Prevents blockchain bloat while maintaining verifiability

**Storage Growth Targets**:
- Pruned node: <100 GB/year storage growth
- Archive node: ~500 GB/year (full history)
- Light client: <1 GB total (just state proofs)
- USSD wallet: 0 bytes (fully custodial micro-node)

